WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.780
<b>Fine Arts Club In Session</b>

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:26.820
GA Geijutsuka Art Design Class

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:04.690
"GA" as a word might not be

00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:06.570
very well known to the public,

00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:09.530
but it means "Geijutsuka Art Design Class"!

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:11.990
Did you make this out of pebbles?

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:15.350
We in the Fine Arts Club
have to produce art work as well.

00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:17.150
So we tried making a 
bunch of variations.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:19.670
Let's take a look at a few!

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:22.230
This one is made simply 
made out of paint tubes.

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:24.140
There are even collages!

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:26.180
This one looks delicious!

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:27.330
And while we're doing this...

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:32.110
GA is starting!

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:29.370
Oh! GA is starting!

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:31.710
I can't believe we get 
screen time in the intro!

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:47.490
Geijutsuka Art Design Class

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:11.940
So, Mizubuchi's group, 
you're doing portraits.

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:15.050
The overall color is looking good, too.

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:16.450
Professor!

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:19.060
Take a look at our piece next!

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:20.720
We didn't have enough 
space in the classroom,

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:22.260
so it's in the hall!

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:28.980
Awara, move your piece immediately.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:30.330
What? Why?

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:33.230
Don't put a trompe l'oeil there.

00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:38.840
<b>Trompe
  L'oeil</b>

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:46.840
Marry me?

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:56.990
Marry me?

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:58.920
Trompe l'oeil?

00:02:58.920 --> 00:02:59.890
Right.

00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:01.780
It means "deceptive art."

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:04.950
Didn't you learn about 
it in middle school?

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:06.320
The one where you 
paint a three dimensional image

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:08.900
on a two dimensional surface 
to create an optical illusion.

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:12.730
Hmm... If I showed you an example,

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:14.900
you'd probably get 
it right away, but...

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:16.310
Namiko-san, Namiko-san.

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:17.580
I have one right here!

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:20.430
Revitalize your face with cosmetics.

00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:22.280
“Secret tips and make–up tricks.”

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:24.460
Wait a second.

00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:26.580
I'll go find someone 
who can actually explain.

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:31.590
For example, Kisaragi-dono, suppose that
 you're appreciating a museum gallery.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:35.680
You see a staff member attempting 
to remove a painting across the hall

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:37.600
but, for some reason, they aren't moving.

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:40.370
Thinking it strange, you approach

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:42.520
and discover that it was not a person,

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:44.410
but a figure painted on the wall.

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:46.210
So this is trompe-l'oeil.

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:48.590
Then you look around and

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:50.570
realize that the other guests, 
are trompe-l'oeil, as well.

00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:51.780
The guests too?!

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:52.640
That's right.

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:56.450
You realize that the only 
person in the museum is you.

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:02.900
Frightened, you attempt 
to leave the gallery.

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:06.610
But, surprise! You discover that
the exit, too, is a trompe-l'oeil.

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:07.460
<b>Ta-da!</b>

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:09.760
Let me out!

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:16.470
Masa. Noda.
 That's probably enough.

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:19.470
<b>Classmate Yoshikawa-san</b>

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:19.470
Miki-chan's group, you guys always get along!

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:21.630
Hiya, Yoshikawa-chi!

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:23.850
O-Oomichi-san, after this...

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:25.110
Yes, I know.

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:26.880
Ah! It's a secret conversation!

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:29.010
Translation: you're going on a date!

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:30.480
Not a date!

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:33.780
<b>Classmate Yoshikawa-chi.</b>

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:31.610
Oomichi-san and I placed first

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:33.790
in that recent exhibition, remember?

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:37.790
They said we have to go to the 
museum to pick up our piece.

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:40.150
The exhibition ended this month.

00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:43.430
Aren't you glad, brother?

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:45.530
They said museum, Kisaragi-chan!

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:47.820
I guess you have no choice but to go!

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:50.300
They really do get along.

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:52.570
<b>Prefectural Museum</b>

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:55.400
So this is the Prefectural Museum.

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:57.580
Yup. It's pretty big inside.

00:04:57.580 --> 00:05:00.290
Kisaragi will have to be careful 
not to get lost, right?

00:05:00.290 --> 00:05:01.850
I'll be fine!

00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:03.210
So says you, Tomokane.

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:05.580
You better not go running off
 or frolicking away either.

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:08.590
I'd never do something that childish!

00:05:08.770 --> 00:05:11.140
Should we have called 
our homeroom teacher?

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:12.990
When destroying art work,

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:14.960
remember to choose relatively
 cheap-looking ones!

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:17.320
Do I really seem that mischievous?

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:21.370
Whoa!! It's huge!

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:23.940
They look like they might
 come out of the painting

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:25.390
and walk around at night.

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:27.170
Now that you mention it,

00:05:27.170 --> 00:05:30.370
last time we were here, that 
person was dancing, wasn't he?

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:31.540
Really?

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:34.180
Then could this white cloth be...

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:36.990
What that naked lady took off!

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:39.150
Can't you be quiet?!

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:43.850
Hmm... I've thought this 
since elementary school:

00:05:43.850 --> 00:05:46.560
there sure are a lot of naked women here.

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:48.790
It sure is awkward passing by all these

00:05:48.790 --> 00:05:51.330
naked women during school 
field trips or gallery visits.

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:52.630
If you go back to the beginning,

00:05:52.630 --> 00:05:54.770
you see that it is fundamental to art.

00:05:55.230 --> 00:05:57.220
The original works of art aimed to express

00:05:57.220 --> 00:06:01.310
a perfect physical form that 
approximated that of a god.

00:06:01.310 --> 00:06:04.580
From Mesopotamia's earthen 
figures to Roman and Greek sculpture.

00:06:04.580 --> 00:06:07.400
So then, it isn't pornographic.

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:09.210
Tomokane-san is brash.

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:12.420
There was also an era in which 
that was the dominant meaning.

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:13.480
Seriously?

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:15.570
After art transitioned from the religious

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:18.760
to part of a noble or aristocratic collection.

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:21.300
While nudity in religious art was okay,

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:25.270
it was considered erotic 
to paint the naked form

00:06:25.270 --> 00:06:26.530
up until the golden era.

00:06:26.530 --> 00:06:29.210
But if you said that it was a goddess

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:31.770
you could safely paint a nude.

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:35.770
These kinds of paintings were 
more popular, so they sold better.

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:39.930
So, nameless artists would 
paint nudes to make money.

00:06:39.930 --> 00:06:43.280
I guess it connects with the 
modern idea of market demand.

00:06:43.490 --> 00:06:45.660
Now that you mention it, 
Professor Sasamoto was also saying...

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:48.970
Professor, do you paint naked 
women because it's fun, too?

00:06:48.970 --> 00:06:51.600
Well of course, if it 
wasn't an enjoyable motif

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:52.790
I wouldn't be inspired by it.

00:06:52.790 --> 00:06:55.130
On the other hand, compared to painting,

00:06:55.130 --> 00:06:59.760
evoking sexual desires in the 
observer is far more rewarding.

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:02.400
So she says.

00:07:02.770 --> 00:07:05.200
I wonder if that's what 
it means to be an artist.

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:07.840
In other words, whether for 
enlightenment or for worldly desires,

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:10.840
the magical power to shake 
a person's soul is...

00:07:11.380 --> 00:07:12.210
Art!

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:15.530
So then, can't it be said
 that even the sinful person's

00:07:15.530 --> 00:07:17.540
pursuit of desire is a type of art?

00:07:17.540 --> 00:07:18.220
Of course!

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:19.820
So...

00:07:20.060 --> 00:07:21.090
Namiko-san!

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:21.610
Strip!

00:07:21.610 --> 00:07:22.500
Like hell!

00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:28.200
This is Yoshikawa-chi's piece?

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:31.830
Yep. I wonder if it's 
okay to just take it?

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:34.340
I just checked, so it should be fine.

00:07:34.340 --> 00:07:35.450
Okay! Let's take it down!

00:07:35.450 --> 00:07:35.970
Right!

00:07:36.220 --> 00:07:38.160
Still, it'll be hard to carry

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:40.330
something this big back all by yourself.

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:43.490
I'm also a little embarrassed 
about walking around town by myself

00:07:43.490 --> 00:07:45.260
with something like this.

00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:47.520
That's why I invited Oomichi-san.

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:48.980
I see, I se-

00:07:48.980 --> 00:07:50.200
Coming through!

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:52.480
Award winning painting coming through!

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:54.180
I'm so sorry, Yoshikawa-san.

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:56.750
The situation has become 
even more embarrassing.

00:07:59.020 --> 00:08:01.490
This large one goes to 
the entrance side, okay?

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:02.030
Okay.

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:04.130
That's not a painting, is it?

00:08:04.770 --> 00:08:05.580
Can I help you?

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:08.820
Oh! No! I just wanted 
to check something...

00:08:09.160 --> 00:08:10.280
Check?

00:08:11.170 --> 00:08:13.370
This oil painting is really pretty!

00:08:13.370 --> 00:08:14.580
Absolutely.

00:08:14.580 --> 00:08:17.480
I wonder how long it takes 
to paint something like this.

00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:21.420
Hey. Looking at paintings, don't 
you just want to touch them?

00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:24.480
Like to feel the bumps 
and grooves of the surface?

00:08:24.740 --> 00:08:28.720
That too, but since exhibition 
pieces tend to be replicas,

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:31.180
I start to want to touch them 
to make sure they're real.

00:08:31.180 --> 00:08:32.990
Hmm, in other words...

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:34.940
It's the same as when you see the

00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:37.000
sample menu items on display 
at the restaurant!

00:08:37.710 --> 00:08:39.400
I completely understand that feeling!

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:41.430
For me, when I look at paintings,

00:08:41.430 --> 00:08:43.030
I have a strong desire to remove them.

00:08:43.030 --> 00:08:44.000
Remove them?

00:08:44.260 --> 00:08:45.990
To look at the wall behind!

00:08:45.990 --> 00:08:47.110
There might be a secret hole,

00:08:47.110 --> 00:08:49.620
or suspicious note pasted 
on the other side!

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:52.080
What, is this a ninja mansion to you?

00:08:53.010 --> 00:08:56.040
Nah, but there might 
just be one hidden...

00:08:56.040 --> 00:08:57.280
You'll get in trouble.

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:00.120
Hey.

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:02.520
What if it's not stuck to the wall,
 but to the painting instead?

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:03.100
No way!

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:03.650
A curse?!

00:09:03.650 --> 00:09:04.520
Get Masa!

00:09:04.850 --> 00:09:07.200
Oh, I see! I'm sorry!

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:09.390
No, no, it's fine. What's wrong?

00:09:09.640 --> 00:09:11.800
Hey, it's Kisaragi and Yoshikawa-san.

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:15.300
Hi, Kyouju-san. 
I've taken down your piece.

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:15.800
Right.

00:09:15.990 --> 00:09:19.200
But Yamaguchi-san was 
just standing there

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:23.310
staring at the paintings and didn't 
even respond when I called.

00:09:23.310 --> 00:09:25.600
Oh, no, I was...

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:31.580
Though there isn't much to see here.

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:33.080
Here?

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:35.890
I was just looking at Kyouju-san's 
and Yoshikawa-san's paintings.

00:09:35.890 --> 00:09:38.860
They're hung up right 
along side professional

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:40.860
or adult work and I was thinking.

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:45.860
I was amazed, jealous, and of course,

00:09:45.860 --> 00:09:47.310
a little frustrated.

00:09:53.060 --> 00:09:54.520
Is something wrong?

00:09:54.520 --> 00:09:56.740
Oh, no. I just wouldn't have expected

00:09:56.740 --> 00:10:00.350
those words to come
 out of your mouth.

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:01.580
Me neither.

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:05.650
I'm sorry.

00:10:05.850 --> 00:10:09.150
No, it's only natural to feel that way.

00:10:09.150 --> 00:10:12.660
After all, you submitted 
your hard work as well.

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:15.720
From what I saw, the one who 
spent the most time

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:18.160
on their piece was Kisaragi-dono.

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:20.310
You're the exact opposite of Noda.

00:10:20.310 --> 00:10:23.170
She's got talent, but she only 
works when she feels like it.

00:10:23.170 --> 00:10:25.870
You're not frustrated 
by others' success,

00:10:25.870 --> 00:10:28.160
but probably by yourself 
for not getting chosen

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:30.010
despite working so hard.

00:10:31.060 --> 00:10:31.940
I see.

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:35.270
You really do love art, don't you?

00:10:38.630 --> 00:10:41.450
I think we should probably 
get out of here soon.

00:10:41.650 --> 00:10:43.350
We'll be in the way of preparations.

00:10:43.350 --> 00:10:44.420
You're right.

00:10:44.420 --> 00:10:46.170
We'd better call Noda 
and Tomokane, as well.

00:10:46.170 --> 00:10:48.560
Oh, I just remembered.

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:50.400
I saw a painting over there

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:52.180
that you would really 
like, Yamaguchi-san.

00:10:52.830 --> 00:10:54.640
I-I would?

00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:55.970
So in that case,

00:10:55.970 --> 00:10:57.740
would it be all right if I borrow 
Yamaguchi-san for a moment?

00:10:57.900 --> 00:10:59.570
As long as you return her.

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:02.570
Can I change her size or density a bit?

00:11:02.570 --> 00:11:03.520
Si-

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:05.350
As long as it's less than 10cm.

00:11:05.350 --> 00:11:06.840
Even Kyouju-san!

00:11:10.150 --> 00:11:12.870
Kyouju, are you going to 
be able to take that home?

00:11:12.870 --> 00:11:14.290
The bus ride might be difficult.

00:11:14.470 --> 00:11:17.980
It might be better to stop by 
school and drop it off there.

00:11:17.980 --> 00:11:20.060
Huh? What's that lying over there?

00:11:22.530 --> 00:11:23.540
Ribbon?

00:11:23.540 --> 00:11:24.580
Yours?

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:32.660
Kisaragi-chan is trapped 
inside the painting!

00:11:32.660 --> 00:11:33.710
That's comically absurd!

00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:38.680
Let's go, Red! 
We've got to save Cyan!

00:11:38.680 --> 00:11:41.280
Understood! Red to the rescue!

00:11:46.580 --> 00:11:48.520
This is trompe-l'oeil as well, right?

00:11:48.850 --> 00:11:50.780
Huh, there are these kinds as well.

00:11:50.780 --> 00:11:52.520
We've been tricked!

00:11:52.520 --> 00:11:55.030
Geijutsuka Art Design Class

00:11:55.030 --> 00:12:00.030
Geijutsuka Art Design Class

00:11:55.030 --> 00:12:00.030
<b>Yoshikawa-chi</b>

00:11:55.030 --> 00:12:00.030
<b> Miki-chan</b>

00:11:55.850 --> 00:11:58.860
Miki-chan's group 
is always getting along!

00:12:00.730 --> 00:12:03.840
I want to become... 
an observant person!

00:12:04.020 --> 00:12:04.740
Huh?

00:12:04.740 --> 00:12:07.200
Observant as in insightful?

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:09.640
I don't really know, 
but something like that.

00:12:09.640 --> 00:12:12.480
For example, being able to figure 
out who designed this uniform,

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:14.990
or who planned this building,

00:12:14.990 --> 00:12:17.370
or what kind of paper is 
used in this sketchbook;

00:12:17.370 --> 00:12:20.500
I want to be a person who can 
see into stuff like that.

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:24.620
Or like that fact that 
Namiko-san tying back her hair

00:12:24.620 --> 00:12:27.470
is really a desperate attempt 
to disguise her bed head.

00:12:27.470 --> 00:12:29.490
You don't have to see into that!

00:12:29.490 --> 00:12:30.390
It must be nice for people

00:12:30.390 --> 00:12:32.140
who have never known the pains 
of naturally curly hair.

00:12:32.140 --> 00:12:34.320
The other day, at my house...

00:12:34.650 --> 00:12:37.270
A Nice Day for Art

00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:40.660
Miki, you're an art student 
so you're studying art, right?

00:12:40.660 --> 00:12:41.640
That's right.

00:12:42.490 --> 00:12:44.350
Do you know what that painting is?

00:12:44.350 --> 00:12:46.370
I see it around a lot.

00:12:46.370 --> 00:12:47.650
Yup. “Sunflower."

00:12:47.650 --> 00:12:49.570
It's a famous masterpiece by Van Gogh.

00:12:50.280 --> 00:12:51.970
<i>This elegant piece by Rosanjin...</i>

00:12:51.050 --> 00:12:51.970
What about this one?

00:12:51.970 --> 00:12:55.150
The Rosan people? 
I wonder where those people are from.

00:12:55.150 --> 00:12:57.080
This surprising appraisal 
after the break!

00:12:57.080 --> 00:12:59.730
Is that hanging scroll real or fake?

00:13:01.850 --> 00:13:04.670
When I think about it, 
I'm in the art design class,

00:13:04.670 --> 00:13:06.960
but I don't know much 
about that kind of art.

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:07.960
Right?

00:13:08.300 --> 00:13:10.820
How to determine the artistic 
value of something?

00:13:10.820 --> 00:13:14.240
You're right, we don't really 
learn much about that in class.

00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:15.700
Oh, well in that case,

00:13:15.700 --> 00:13:18.380
since it might be informative, 
do you want to go to this?

00:13:18.610 --> 00:13:20.430
It came with a book I bought.

00:13:20.840 --> 00:13:23.010
Is that a discount ticket 
to an exhibition?

00:13:23.010 --> 00:13:27.520
Since I haven't had the chance 
to see many famous pieces...

00:13:23.030 --> 00:13:27.520
 20th Century Japanese Art
Sat, 8/22 ~ Wed, 9/30

00:13:27.520 --> 00:13:29.080
How about on our day off?

00:13:29.080 --> 00:13:29.880
Sure.

00:13:29.880 --> 00:13:32.770
In order to be insightful, we'll 
need to observe the real thing first.

00:13:32.770 --> 00:13:33.460
Right!

00:13:33.730 --> 00:13:35.590
I'm gonna give it a shot!

00:13:35.780 --> 00:13:37.490
Kisaragi-chan! Nako-san!

00:13:37.490 --> 00:13:39.420
No shortening my name.

00:13:39.860 --> 00:13:42.370
<b>City Art Museum</b>

00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:44.930
So here we are, at the 
museum entrance on Sunday.

00:13:44.930 --> 00:13:47.010
I'm glad everyone's here,

00:13:47.010 --> 00:13:49.040
but why are we all in uniform?

00:13:49.040 --> 00:13:51.920
But, didn't they tell us 
in homeroom on Friday?

00:13:51.920 --> 00:13:53.380
Today, teachers are looking

00:13:53.380 --> 00:13:55.800
around the station for 
misbehaving students.

00:13:55.800 --> 00:13:57.520
This is just a precaution.

00:13:57.520 --> 00:13:59.680
And you're in your uniform because...?

00:13:59.680 --> 00:14:03.280
Oh, I was at school this morning 
picking up my assignment.

00:14:03.280 --> 00:14:07.020
Um, five <i>High School</i> students, please!

00:14:07.020 --> 00:14:10.640
She definitely emphasized High 
School just now, didn't she?

00:14:10.640 --> 00:14:14.530
I see. She's wearing 
her uniform out of pride.

00:14:14.860 --> 00:14:17.140
This is the first time we've 
been in this museum, right?

00:14:17.140 --> 00:14:20.540
The last one we went to was 
owned by the prefecture.

00:14:20.720 --> 00:14:22.840
I wonder where they're 
holding the exhibition.

00:14:23.560 --> 00:14:25.960
Hikoyasu Yoshikazu's 
original exhibition!

00:14:25.960 --> 00:14:27.160
Let's go to this one!

00:14:27.160 --> 00:14:28.530
That's next month.

00:14:28.530 --> 00:14:32.550
Not this time! We came here 
to learn about modern art.

00:14:34.070 --> 00:14:39.680
Deep Brunya Exhibition
Rabbit Ears, Rabbit Tails {Ref: Dick Bruna}

00:14:34.260 --> 00:14:38.270
Kisaragi-dono's heart has been 
captured by the picture book exhibit.

00:14:38.520 --> 00:14:41.560
No, Kisaragi-chan! 
We're not going to that one today!

00:14:39.710 --> 00:14:41.560
<b>Wanna~</b>

00:14:41.580 --> 00:14:49.820
Children's Picture Contest
Award Winning Work

00:14:41.770 --> 00:14:44.340
So, where do we hand in our tickets?

00:14:44.340 --> 00:14:46.930
Noda, that looks like 
the kids' corner.

00:14:46.930 --> 00:14:49.610
Why did we come here again?

00:14:49.850 --> 00:14:51.840
<b> To See
20th Century
Japanese Art</b>

00:14:50.340 --> 00:14:51.840
Please enjoy your visit.

00:14:52.120 --> 00:14:53.970
Quiet inside the exhibit.

00:15:00.520 --> 00:15:02.470
Um... Noda-chan?

00:15:02.470 --> 00:15:03.890
Are you looking carefully?

00:15:03.890 --> 00:15:05.080
Are you appreciating it?

00:15:05.080 --> 00:15:06.840
Yup! I'm looking carefully!

00:15:06.840 --> 00:15:08.050
I'm looking very closely!

00:15:08.050 --> 00:15:08.860
Liar!

00:15:08.860 --> 00:15:10.090
You're the girl who spends eight minutes

00:15:10.090 --> 00:15:12.360
on every page of a fashion 
magazine, so try a little harder!

00:15:12.650 --> 00:15:15.860
<i>The female figure portrayed in the 
left hand corner of the painting...</i>

00:15:16.200 --> 00:15:17.320
What's that?

00:15:17.320 --> 00:15:19.190
Oh, that's an audio guided tour.

00:15:19.190 --> 00:15:22.130
Using that you can listen to 
commentary on the art work, right?

00:15:22.130 --> 00:15:24.140
That's cool! 
I want one!

00:15:24.140 --> 00:15:25.010
I want that!

00:15:25.010 --> 00:15:26.470
Fine.

00:15:26.470 --> 00:15:29.150
Tomokane, you were using a 
CD player earlier, right?

00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:34.020
They charge you for a real one,
 so live with this, okay?

00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:37.110
Completed in 1925, this piece 
depicts a church in Paris...

00:15:35.300 --> 00:15:37.110
The headphones are just for effect.

00:15:37.110 --> 00:15:40.420
Besides, she's probably more 
informative than the audio guide anyway.

00:15:41.670 --> 00:15:43.950
Oh, the artist of this work...

00:15:43.950 --> 00:15:45.320
You know him?

00:15:45.910 --> 00:15:47.380
I don't really know him,

00:15:47.380 --> 00:15:49.670
but my uncle received 
some paintings from him.

00:15:49.670 --> 00:15:50.550
Two of them.

00:15:51.460 --> 00:15:54.440
Apparently, the artist 
passed away a few years ago

00:15:54.440 --> 00:15:56.990
and the value of the 
paintings has gone way up.

00:15:58.040 --> 00:16:00.000
So you mean this is a 
valuable piece as well?!

00:16:00.180 --> 00:16:01.630
Hmm... I wonder how much it costs.

00:16:01.630 --> 00:16:04.200
Tomokane-san, how much do 
you think this pieces costs?

00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:05.300
Hmm... let's see.

00:16:05.300 --> 00:16:08.070
One billion? 
No, maybe ten billion?

00:16:08.070 --> 00:16:09.980
So you're finally interested in art,

00:16:10.270 --> 00:16:11.410
because of money...

00:16:11.960 --> 00:16:13.960
I've heard that one way to analyze art

00:16:13.960 --> 00:16:18.000
is to “look at art as if
 you intend to buy it.”

00:16:18.850 --> 00:16:21.060
That does sound interesting, as well.

00:16:21.280 --> 00:16:23.910
Oh! I like this piece.

00:16:23.910 --> 00:16:25.240
It's a collage, isn't it?

00:16:25.240 --> 00:16:27.890
It sure is pretty, but...

00:16:27.890 --> 00:16:30.480
I feel like I'd be able to make it myself.

00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:32.790
I don't think I'd pay money for it.

00:16:32.790 --> 00:16:34.160
What about this one?

00:16:34.160 --> 00:16:36.360
Nah, I wouldn't buy this painting.

00:16:36.360 --> 00:16:38.740
I'm better than this. Probably.

00:16:38.740 --> 00:16:41.810
I'd probably be able to copy this one.

00:16:42.120 --> 00:16:44.180
Somehow our perspective has changed from

00:16:44.180 --> 00:16:47.850
“buying this piece” to 
“being able to paint this piece."

00:16:47.850 --> 00:16:50.470
Even though I guess it's 
natural for an art student,

00:16:50.470 --> 00:16:53.830
there seems to be a 
lot of overconfidence.

00:16:56.360 --> 00:16:58.600
Rodin's “The Thinker."

00:16:58.600 --> 00:17:00.800
There are pieces like that,

00:17:00.800 --> 00:17:02.720
which only use pose 
as an expression of art.

00:17:02.720 --> 00:17:03.820
I guess.

00:17:03.820 --> 00:17:05.080
That's a funny expression.

00:17:05.080 --> 00:17:08.060
It would depend on how well the 
piece is recognized by the public.

00:17:08.060 --> 00:17:09.870
For example, do you recognize this?

00:17:10.830 --> 00:17:12.910
I feel like I've seen it before...

00:17:12.910 --> 00:17:14.600
Michaelangelo's “La Pieta."

00:17:15.110 --> 00:17:17.980
Hmm, the title doesn't 
really ring a bell.

00:17:17.980 --> 00:17:18.630
Right?

00:17:18.630 --> 00:17:20.890
I actually like this one a lot...

00:17:21.070 --> 00:17:22.960
Ares Borghese.

00:17:23.400 --> 00:17:25.020
The Bust of Eros.

00:17:25.020 --> 00:17:26.750
The Discobolos of Myron

00:17:27.230 --> 00:17:30.860
I can't tell them apart 
without seeing the real thing.

00:17:31.290 --> 00:17:35.100
It's not just sculptures; there are
 many paintings like that as well.

00:17:35.100 --> 00:17:37.460
Oh! Manet's “Young Flautist."

00:17:37.460 --> 00:17:39.000
A copy of Manet!

00:17:39.560 --> 00:17:41.400
“The Scream” by Munch!

00:17:42.050 --> 00:17:43.710
I knew you'd do that one.

00:17:44.060 --> 00:17:46.210
Raphael's “The Seated Madonna."

00:17:46.470 --> 00:17:50.210
“Sunshu Ejiri” from Hokusai's 
“Fugaku Sanjurokkei."

00:17:50.210 --> 00:17:54.450
Whew. I feel like I've been 
completely immersed in art.

00:17:54.450 --> 00:17:56.520
We know more than you'd expect, right?

00:17:56.730 --> 00:18:00.350
We can relate anything 
back to a famous work!

00:18:00.350 --> 00:18:01.030
Hey, hey.

00:18:01.290 --> 00:18:04.770
Like the you're "Nio Guardian: Ungyo"!

00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:09.410
Not so! You're the central figure 
of Botticelli's “Primavera."

00:18:13.440 --> 00:18:14.200
<b>Crash!</b>

00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:14.200
<b>Bang!</b>

00:18:14.200 --> 00:18:14.940
<b>Crunch!</b>

00:18:14.610 --> 00:18:16.550
“Lion Hunt” by Rubens.

00:18:16.840 --> 00:18:17.570
<b>Crash!</b>

00:18:17.210 --> 00:18:17.570
<b>Bang!</b>

00:18:17.570 --> 00:18:18.340
<b>Crunch!</b>

00:18:18.340 --> 00:18:21.210
Tsukioka Yoshitoshi's 
"Youkai Art" (late period).

00:18:24.650 --> 00:18:27.380
Hey. That's Professor Usami over there!

00:18:27.380 --> 00:18:28.920
Is she on the lookout for 
misbehaving students?

00:18:28.920 --> 00:18:31.490
Teacher! Professor Usami!

00:18:31.610 --> 00:18:34.490
My! It's you guys.

00:18:34.490 --> 00:18:38.670
But... calling me “Professor” 
outside of school is a little...

00:18:38.670 --> 00:18:40.600
Oh! So you're not on patrol.

00:18:40.950 --> 00:18:43.430
Is that what a teacher is?

00:18:43.430 --> 00:18:46.270
Well of course, teachers 
have a private life as well.

00:18:46.270 --> 00:18:48.010
Like friends or lovers.

00:18:48.010 --> 00:18:51.910
On days off she just wants to 
switch off from being a teacher.

00:18:52.920 --> 00:18:56.510
Wearing a suit over running shoes 
in public... could that be...

00:18:56.750 --> 00:18:58.310
one of the same profession!

00:19:01.250 --> 00:19:04.970
It's really great that you guys
 have an interest in modern art.

00:19:04.970 --> 00:19:07.790
Or rather, we came in order to 
develop an interest in modern art.

00:19:07.790 --> 00:19:11.410
To be honest, I still don't 
 understand it very well.

00:19:11.410 --> 00:19:12.800
That might be so.

00:19:12.800 --> 00:19:14.480
With a bit of experience and knowledge,

00:19:14.480 --> 00:19:16.920
artistic appreciation 
becomes more entertaining.

00:19:16.920 --> 00:19:19.300
Like in art history, 
or an artist's biography.

00:19:19.830 --> 00:19:23.630
I think that it's important to look 
at plenty of different art when you can.

00:19:23.630 --> 00:19:26.580
Especially for you guys 
who are 15 or 16 right?

00:19:26.580 --> 00:19:31.040
I feel like there are so many 
things that only the young sense.

00:19:31.040 --> 00:19:32.530
Blade catch!

00:19:33.040 --> 00:19:35.100
Like their sense?

00:19:35.410 --> 00:19:36.850
Yes, of course!

00:19:37.770 --> 00:19:41.860
Art appreciation is the same as 
watching TV or reading a book.

00:19:42.680 --> 00:19:47.890
If you can find a few that you like 
or that leave a lasting impression,

00:19:47.890 --> 00:19:51.070
I think you've gained a 
good appreciation of art.

00:19:51.200 --> 00:19:53.690
In that case I'd have to 
go for the billion piece.

00:19:54.270 --> 00:19:55.570
What?! One billion?!

00:19:55.790 --> 00:19:59.150
Was there a Picasso or something?

00:19:59.150 --> 00:20:01.580
We'll see you tomorrow, Professor!

00:20:01.600 --> 00:20:10.970
<b> 2nd Exhibition Room 2E
The 58th Independence Writing...</b>

00:20:02.050 --> 00:20:05.360
So, Noda, what was your 
impression of art appreciation?

00:20:05.360 --> 00:20:07.620
Hmm... as someone who 
had some experience with art,

00:20:07.620 --> 00:20:10.610
I thought I would understand 
it a little more than I did.

00:20:11.150 --> 00:20:14.310
Modern art is a particularly difficult genre.

00:20:14.310 --> 00:20:17.100
Next time it might be better to choose 
something easier to understand.

00:20:17.470 --> 00:20:18.750
That's true.

00:20:18.750 --> 00:20:21.770
If we match it with their level...

00:20:21.780 --> 00:20:25.270
Children's Picture Contest
Award Winning Work

00:20:25.510 --> 00:20:27.550
This one on the right is a dragonfly.

00:20:27.550 --> 00:20:28.770
Or a bird?

00:20:28.970 --> 00:20:31.040
Ah-ha! This must be a car!

00:20:32.120 --> 00:20:35.280
This bold use of green is so vivid!

00:20:35.840 --> 00:20:38.990
My, my. What bright futures 
these children have.

00:20:42.080 --> 00:20:44.190
Whoa! It's already 3:30!

00:20:44.370 --> 00:20:48.010
Just standing around and looking 
can take up a lot of time!

00:20:48.600 --> 00:20:50.530
Hey! Our teachers are here!

00:20:50.530 --> 00:20:52.700
They really were on patrol!

00:20:52.870 --> 00:20:54.890
It must be tough, 
even on their day off.

00:20:54.890 --> 00:20:56.170
Hey! Professor Sotom-

00:20:56.170 --> 00:20:56.820
Hold on.

00:20:56.820 --> 00:20:58.410
Professor Usami told us we need

00:20:58.410 --> 00:21:01.210
to refrain from using 
“Professor” outside of school.

00:21:01.410 --> 00:21:03.300
Takuma-san!

00:21:03.880 --> 00:21:05.980
Sotoma Takuma-san!

00:21:05.980 --> 00:21:08.250
Keep up the good work!

00:21:08.250 --> 00:21:09.570
Takuma-san!

00:21:09.570 --> 00:21:12.410
Do they really hate me that much?

00:21:16.860 --> 00:21:18.280
Morning, Noda.

00:21:19.340 --> 00:21:20.450
What's that book?

00:21:20.450 --> 00:21:22.640
I thought I'd take what Professor Usami said

00:21:22.640 --> 00:21:25.180
and try to find more 
artists that I like.

00:21:25.590 --> 00:21:28.730
Until then, I'll put off 
trying to see through art.

00:21:28.730 --> 00:21:33.240
Well, after all, if it was easy to do 
we wouldn't be attending this school.

00:21:33.240 --> 00:21:34.340
Good morning.

00:21:34.340 --> 00:21:35.250
Oh, Masa.

00:21:35.250 --> 00:21:36.030
Good morning!

00:21:39.940 --> 00:21:40.850
That person over there,

00:21:40.850 --> 00:21:43.410
in the British trench coat 
and the enamel pin heel

00:21:43.410 --> 00:21:45.290
of foreign brand and ostrich 
bag of limited collaboration

00:21:45.290 --> 00:21:48.270
with a famous designer and brand 
new off the line this year!

00:21:49.040 --> 00:21:55.010
<b> Seen through person.</b>

00:21:49.380 --> 00:21:52.910
I changed my mind. You'll definitely 
become an observant woman.

00:21:52.910 --> 00:21:54.540
If you actually try.

00:23:25.070 --> 00:23:40.060
<b>Next Episode</b>

00:23:25.640 --> 00:23:27.550
Surrealism began in 1920 
with Andre Brenton's...

00:23:27.550 --> 00:23:29.830
Kisaragi! Wake up!

00:23:29.830 --> 00:23:33.900
I'm weak after meals and PE...

00:23:33.900 --> 00:23:36.060
I give up, she just won't move.

00:23:36.060 --> 00:23:38.130
Kisaragi-chan, wake up.

00:23:36.080 --> 00:23:40.060
Surrealism
